{"records":[{"id":"jhzmfr8bbs","title":"A replisome-associated histone H3-H4 chaperone required for epigenetic inheritance","description":"Faithful transfer of parental histones to newly replicated daughter DNA strands is critical for inheritance of epigenetic states. Although replication proteins that facilitate parental histone transfer have been identified, how intact histone H3-H4 tetramers travel from the front to the back of the replication fork remains unknown. Here, we use AlphaFold- Multimer structural predictions combined with biochemical and genetic approaches to identify the Mrc1/CLASPIN subunit of the replisome as a histone chaperone. Mrc1 contains a conserved histone binding domain that forms a brace around the H3-H4 tetramer mimicking nucleosomal DNA and H2A-H2B histones, is required for heterochromatin inheritance, and promotes parental histone recycling during replication. We further identify binding sites for the FACT histone chaperone in Swi1/TIMELESS and DNA polymerase α that are required for heterochromatin inheritance. We propose that Mrc1, in concert with FACT acting as a mobile co-chaperone, coordinates the distribution of parental histones to newly replicated DNA.\n\nThis dataset is associated with the article \"A replisome-associated histone H3-H4 chaperone required for epigenetic inheritance\".  It contains all yeast spotting assays, protein gels, western blots, and scripts to generate AlphaFold screen predictions using ColabFold-batch.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-31T07:41:59.71Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jhzmfr8bbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jhzmfr8bbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Juntao","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yujie","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yimeng","last_name":"Fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joao","last_name":"Paulo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dadmehr","last_name":"Yaghoubi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xu","last_name":"Hua","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gergana","last_name":"Shipkovenska","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Takenori","last_name":"Toda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiguo","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Steven","last_name":"Gygi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Songtao","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qing","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danesh","last_name":"Moazed","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wp5mnc3gbc","title":"Huang, YH et al., 2020-01","description":"Source data; Huang, YH et al, 2020; Structure-Based Mechanisms of a Molecular RNA Polymerase/Chaperone Machine Required for Ribosome Biosynthesis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-28T16:22:05.699Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wp5mnc3gbc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wp5mnc3gbc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Wahl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Structural Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6jf6f85d5j","title":"CnoX Is a Chaperedoxin: A Holdase that Protects Its Substrates from Irreversible Oxidation","description":"CnoX Is a Chaperedoxin: A Holdase that Protects Its Substrates from Irreversible Oxidation- Additional Figures (Triplicates)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-26T10:53:42.583Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6jf6f85d5j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6jf6f85d5j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Camille","last_name":"Goemans","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Chaperone Protein","Redox Process"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"7nx4c8s76r","title":"Recruitment of tetraspanin TSP-15 regulates plasma membrane repair in C. elegans epidermis","description":"Maintaining the integrity of the plasma membrane after cellular damage is essential for cell survival. However, it is unclear how cells repair large membrane injuries in vivo. In this study, we report that the tetraspanin protein, TSP-15, is recruited to large membrane wounds and forms a ring-like structure in C. elegans syncytial epidermis and promotes membrane repair after an injury. TSP-15 recruits from the adjacent region underneath the plasma membrane to the wound site in a RAB-5-dependent manner upon membrane damage. Genetic and live imaging analysis suggested that the endosomal sorting complex required for transport III (ESCRT III) is essential for recruiting TSP-15 from the early endosome to the damaged membrane. Moreover, TSP-15 interacts with and is required for the accumulation of the t-SNARE protein Syntaxin-2, which facilitates membrane repair. These findings suggest that recruiting TSP-15 to the wounded membrane provides novel insights into the cellular repair of large wounds resulting from environmental insults. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-27T20:20:19.854Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7nx4c8s76r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7nx4c8s76r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suhong","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bydb7fhnr3","title":"Inositol 6 phosphate is a required cofactor for pyrin inflammasome activation","description":"FACS plots","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-16T11:59:53.13Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bydb7fhnr3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bydb7fhnr3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"thomas","last_name":"henry","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Dalmon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah2","last_name":"Dalmon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cf5vbhy67d","title":"Human SMARCA5 is continuously required for Nucleosome Spacing","description":"Western blot images from Figure 1A. The top half is an immunoblot with anti-SMARCA5 and anti-FLAG and the bottom half is an immunoblot with anti-GAPDH. On a PDVF membrane. Grayscale image of IR700 or IR800 channel via LICOR Odyssey. Precision plus blue protein ladder. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-13T07:31:14.955Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cf5vbhy67d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cf5vbhy67d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Monica","last_name":"Bomber","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"srfczk4whb","title":"RNA-RNA interactomes of ProQ and Hfq reveal overlapping and competing roles","description":"Base pairing RNAs modulate gene expression in all studied organisms. In many bacteria, the base pairing between most small regulatory RNAs (sRNAs) and their targets is mediated by the Hfq RNA chaperone. However, recent studies have shown FinO-domain proteins also bind sRNAs. To examine the global contribution of the FinO-domain ProQ protein in Escherichia coli, we carried out RIL-seq to identify RNA pairs bound to this protein. The RNA-RNA interactome for ProQ contains hundreds of pairs. Intriguingly, a significant fraction of the ProQ-bound RNA pairs also are found associated with Hfq, indicating overlapping, complementary or competing roles for the two proteins. Characterization of one novel RNA pair bound by both chaperones revealed that while Hfq is required for RNA sponge-mediated down regulation of the sRNA, ProQ can inhibit this regulation. Overall our results uncover increased complexity in RNA regulatory networks involving RNA chaperone proteins, RNases, sRNAs and mRNAs. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-03T22:31:51.31Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/srfczk4whb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/srfczk4whb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gisela","last_name":"Storz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sahar","last_name":"Melamed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philip","last_name":"Adams","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aixia","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hongen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bacteria","RNA","RNA-Binding Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gpf74k8v9x","title":"Dr Gunaseelan Manuscript APB data","description":"Pterostilbene, a dietary polyphenol modulates PI3K/AkT/GLUT4 signaling in the liver of high fat diet and low dose Streptozotocin induced type 2 diabetic C57BL/6J mice","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-03T07:52:40.688Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gpf74k8v9x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gpf74k8v9x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Leelevinothan","last_name":"pari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences","Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mskwggm4tx","title":"The mechanosensitive TRPV4 is required for crystal-induced inflammation","description":"The original data for all figures and supplement figures","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-20T18:37:21.131Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mskwggm4tx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mskwggm4tx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lvyi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Behavioral Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"csn2tf7c9r","title":"An unconvention